.TH RADIO 3
.SH NAME
radio \- packet radio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a '#W' /dev
.PP
.B /dev/radio0/data
.br
.B /dev/radio0/ctl
.br
.B /dev/radio0/stat
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I radio
device serves a directory per radio interface, each holding
.BR data ,
.B ctl
and
.BR stat .
The driver behind it is for the Semtech SX127x family in LoRa mode \- the
part an HopeRF RFM9x module is built around \- reached over SPI.
.PP
.B Data
is message-delimited.  One write is one packet on the air; one read is
one packet received, blocking until there is one.  It is not a byte
stream, because a radio is not one and because what is layered above
depends on the boundary surviving:
.IR ssl (3)
is a filter over a file descriptor, and Styx is a request and a reply.
.PP
Two things are refused rather than accommodated.
.TP
.B "a short read"
A read whose buffer is smaller than the waiting packet fails, naming the
size the packet needs, and the packet stays queued for a read that fits.
It is not truncated.
.IR Ssl (3)
states the contract this inherits \- as long as reads specify buffers of
equal or greater length than the writes at the other end, one write
corresponds to one read \- and a device that quietly returned the first
.I n
bytes would break it somewhere else entirely.
.TP
.B "an oversize write"
A write longer than the payload budget fails and names the budget.  It
is not split across two packets.  Splitting is a segmentation layer, and
a device that did it silently would hide the question of what message
size a link can carry.
.PP
The
.B iounit
of
.B data
is that budget, which is 251 bytes: 255 of FIFO less the four-byte
RadioHead header.  That matters beyond documentation, because
.I exportfs
adopts a transport's iounit when negotiating a Styx message size, so a
server over this settles on a radio-sized message without being told.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes one command per write:
.TP
.BI freq " hz"
Carrier frequency.
.TP
.BI power " dbm"
Transmit power, 2 to 17, through the PA_BOOST output an RFM9x bonds out.
.TP
.BI sf " n"
Spreading factor, 6 to 12.
.TP
.BI bw " hz"
Bandwidth; one of the values the part has, 7800 through 500000.
.TP
.BI cr " n"
Coding rate denominator, 5 to 8.
.TP
.BI node " n"
This station's RadioHead address.
.TP
.BI dest " n"
The default destination address; 255 is broadcast.
.TP
.B reset
Put the part back in sleep and reapply the settings.
.PP
.B Stat
returns the parameters in force, the RSSI and SNR of the last packet
received, and counters for packets sent and received, CRC errors and
oversize writes.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/port/devradio.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/port/sx127x.c
.br
.B /os/inferno/mps2/spipl022.c
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A kernel with no radio fitted serves an empty
.B #W
directory; the driver says so on the console at boot, naming the
RegVersion it read, because a bus that is not reaching the part reads
.B 0x00
or
.B 0xff
and those are worth telling apart from a different chip.
.SH BUGS
Acknowledgement and retry are not implemented.  The RadioHead four-byte
header is sent and parsed, and a packet not addressed to this station or
to the broadcast address is dropped, but
.B _RH_FLAGS_ACK
is never set or waited for, so a lost packet stays lost.
.PP
Only one interface exists.  A PyCubed has two radio sockets on one SPI
bus, RF1 and RF2; this drives the first.
.PP
The only board this runs on is
.BR mps2 ,
under a QEMU patched to model an SX1276 on its Shield0 SPI header
.RB ( tools/qemu-patches ,
0027 to 0029).  That is a fixture: an MPS2 has the expansion header and
no radio on it, and a PyCubed wires the same part to a SAMD51 SERCOM
instead.  So what is tested here is the register, FIFO and interrupt
sequencing and the file interface above it, and not any real board's
bus.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radiochk (1),
.IR ssl (3)
